Based on the analysis of the error log and source code, the issue is that `CONFIG_STDOUT_CONSOLE` is a feature specific to the minimal C library (`MINIMAL_LIBC`), but it is being enabled unconditionally in test configurations that may use other C libraries like Newlib or Picolibc. This causes a Kconfig warning because the dependency on `MINIMAL_LIBC` is not met.

A previous attempt to fix this by simply removing the dependency from the Kconfig file resulted in a runtime crash. This is because the implementation file for `STDOUT_CONSOLE` (`stdout_console.c`) defines standard C library functions (like `fputc`, `puts`, etc.), which conflict with the implementations in a full C library.

The correct fix is to make `CONFIG_STDOUT_CONSOLE` a general-purpose option that can be safely enabled with any C library, but ensure its underlying implementation only takes effect when `MINIMAL_LIBC` is used. This resolves the Kconfig warning without causing runtime conflicts.

This is achieved by:
1.  Removing the `depends on MINIMAL_LIBC` from the `CONFIG_STDOUT_CONSOLE` definition in `lib/libc/Kconfig`. This allows the option to be enabled with any C library, resolving the warning.
2.  In `lib/libc/minimal/source/stdout/stdout_console.c`, wrapping the entire implementation inside an `#ifdef CONFIG_MINIMAL_LIBC` block.
3.  Providing a dummy (no-op) implementation of `__stdout_hook_install()` for the case where a different C library is used. This is necessary to satisfy the linker, as other parts of the system (like the console driver) will call this function when `CONFIG_STDOUT_CONSOLE` is enabled. The dummy implementation ensures that for full C libraries, which have their own stdout handling, the minimal libc hook mechanism is not used.

This approach makes the configuration system more robust and fixes the root cause of both the Kconfig warning and the potential runtime crash.

